@*
    Spell-sigil sprite — the 20 <symbol> marks of the spell-card deck, lifted verbatim
    from the design reference's <defs> sprite
    (_work/shipped/spell-cards/assets/design-v5/Spell Cards.dc.html):
    the 14 spell marks, the shared reference tome (sig-tome), the 4 pack marks
    (sig-spellbook, sig-umbraco17, sig-cloud, sig-dotnet) and sig-reference.

    Geometry is code; assignment is content. Editors pick WHICH mark a spell card
    wears (the cardMark dropdown); they never author drawing instructions, which is
    what keeps the stored-XSS surface closed. Consume a mark as:
        <svg class="…" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#sig-explore"></use></svg>
    The consuming <svg> is aria-hidden and carries NO role="img", aria-label, <title>
    or <desc> — the card button's own aria-label is the sole accessible name, and a
    second name source inside the shadow tree would compete with it. For the same
    reason the <symbol>s below stay title-less; a <title> is imported into the shadow
    tree at every <use>.

    Renders at most once per request — the guard below makes that structural rather
    than a convention a caller has to remember, since two deck blocks on one page
    would otherwise emit 21 duplicate ids. Step 6 owns all the CSS (sizing, and the
    --sig-play gate for the sig-draw / sig-pulse / sig-blink / … motion classes that
    the shapes below already carry).

    COLOUR MECHANISM — do not "simplify" this:
      * Structure and texture stay the LITERAL #F0EDE8. Leave them literal.
      * Each mark carries exactly ONE accent shape, fill="currentColor". (sig-tome is
        the deliberate exception: the shared reference mark is fully monochrome, as it
        is in the reference.)
      * The consuming <svg> tints that shape by setting `color`. `color`/`currentColor`
        is the ONLY channel that crosses into a <use> shadow tree.

    Two mechanisms that DO NOT WORK — both were tried; do not re-try them:
      1. A `--sig-accent` custom property on the consuming <svg>. The value resolves on
         the <svg> itself but does not reach the <use> shadow tree, so the accent shape
         silently falls back to its literal default. True whether set inline or by a
         class rule.
      2. `fill` on the consuming <svg>, with the accent shape inheriting it. Also does
         not cross into the shadow tree.

    The sprite is hidden with position:absolute;width:0;height:0;overflow:hidden and is
    NOT display:none — display:none stops <use> resolving in some engines.
*@
@{
    // Emit at most once per request: the sprite defines 21 global ids, so a second
    // render would be invalid HTML and ~15KB of dead payload.
    const string SpriteRenderedKey = "_SpellSigilsRendered";
    if (Context.Items.ContainsKey(SpriteRenderedKey)) { return; }
    Context.Items[SpriteRenderedKey] = true;
}
